  "sharedConclusions": [
    "All four controlled scenarios preserve the device code across settings tabs and refresh without sending a second login; the two receipt phases produce compatible pending states.",
    "With storage scheduling alone, the original one-second query timed out before the handoff; the unchanged production code displayed it after the subsequent legitimate completion.",
    "A matching subset of the old Windows DOM is observed both before and after the accepted write begins, so those Windows substeps cannot be ordered from the hosted DOM alone.",
    "The login mutation has three observed writing transactions (add, update, consume). Whole-page scheduler callback and transaction totals also include initial reads and independent useOperations subscribers. They are not login request or login-write counts."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Windows did not instrument its IndexedDB tasks or HTTP dispatch chronology. The 16 ms control demonstrates sufficiency, not the measured Windows timer latency.",
    "Elapsed times are observations of this controlled macOS test process. This is not a timing claim about real IndexedDB or a live sign-in service."
  ],
